# Analyst Notes

Rundll32 is one of the most commonly abused Windows LOLBins because it allows adversaries to execute malicious DLLs while appearing to use a trusted Microsoft binary. This technique is frequently observed in malware families, red-team frameworks, and post-exploitation toolkits.

While legitimate Rundll32 activity is expected within Windows environments, executions involving unusual DLL paths, suspicious parent processes, or uncommon command-line arguments should be treated as high-priority investigations.

This detection should be correlated with:

- File Creation (Sysmon Event ID 11)
- Registry Modification (Sysmon Event ID 13)
- PowerShell Execution
- Scheduled Task Creation
- Service Creation
- Network Connections

to build a comprehensive view of attacker activity.


